<h2>The occurrence and the accusation</h2>
<p>The informant\'s case was stark: intercepted near a house by six accused, assaulted, confined, his phone snatched, filmed at gunpoint, and threatened over evidence he was to give in another proceeding. Within the same narrative, however, lay the detail that decided this application — the rod blow that caused the head and eye injuries was the act of one co-accused. For the remaining accused, the allegations were collective: part of the group, present at the scene, acting with common intention under Section 34 IPC.</p>

<h2>Role attribution: the decisive axis of pre-arrest bail</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>The accused with the overt act</strong><p>Faces the gravamen of the injury-causing allegation; medical opinion on the grievous injury attaches to his act; anticipatory bail is correspondingly harder, and custodial interrogation more defensible.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>The accused named collectively</strong><p>Carries a vicarious allegation through Section 34 IPC. Clean antecedents, the absence of an individual overt act, and indications of a mutual clash — here, a registered counter-case — make pre-arrest protection a realistic outcome.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The counter-case as context</h2>
<p>Cross-cases from a single occurrence are a familiar feature of rural land and family altercations: each side registers its own FIR, and each side\'s accused become the other side\'s informants. Courts treat the existence of a counter-case with care — it does not absolve anyone, but it reframes the incident as a two-sided clash rather than a one-directional attack, which bears directly on the necessity of custodial interrogation. Here, Srinagar P.S. Case No. 19 of 2023 stood registered against the informant\'s side, and the court recorded it among the circumstances weighed in granting protection.</p>

<h2>Conditions and consequences</h2>
<p>The order follows the standard Patna architecture: protection operates if the petitioner is arrested or surrenders within six weeks; bonds of ₹10,000 with two sureties; and the conditions of Section 482(2) BNSS apply — the petitioner must make himself available for interrogation as required, must not directly or indirectly induce, threaten or promise anything to any person acquainted with the facts so as to dissuade them from disclosing them to the court or police, and must not leave India without prior permission where that condition is imposed. Anticipatory bail orders of this kind are enforceable by their terms: a breach, or conduct amounting to interference with the investigation, exposes the accused to cancellation and immediate arrest.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for practitioners</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Map every overt act in the FIR to its named author before drafting — the gap between the group allegation and the individual role is the argument. Place antecedent verification on record affirmatively rather than waiting for the State\'s report. Where a counter-case exists, annex its FIR and injury reports; symmetry of injuries is often more persuasive than rhetoric. And advise clients that a six-week surrender window is a deadline, not a suggestion.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>For offences committed on or after 1 July 2024, the corresponding provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 apply — notably Section 109 (attempt to murder) in place of Section 307 IPC and Section 3(5) for common intention — while anticipatory bail itself is governed by Section 482 BNSS in place of Section 438 CrPC.</p>
</div>
